From a7a033f834a793ab13046bd409b80f27a606c63e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: William Emerison Six <billsix@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 8 Sep 2026 03:25:27 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 329/488] soh: name the 11 address-named symbols defined in
 z_en_ik.c

SoH carries the Ocarina of Time decomp, in which several thousand symbols are
still named after their ROM address. This commit names every such symbol DEFINED
IN ONE FILE, so the work can be reviewed a file at a time.

The file names the unit; it does not bound it. Each rename is applied wherever
the symbol occurs -- the declaration in the header, the definition, and every
call site -- so this commit reaches into whatever other files use these symbols
and leaves no dangling reference. It is a pure rename: no behaviour changes, and
the only edits that are not renames are the provenance comments described below.

Two name sources are used, and every name says which it came from:

  * zeldaret/oot -- SoH's decomp IS zeldaret/oot at the same ROM addresses, so
    where oot has already named a symbol, that name is adopted verbatim. The
    definition gets a "Name from zeldaret/oot" comment and every declaration a
    short "// was <addr> [oot]" tag.

  * DEDUCED -- oot leaves a great many symbols address-named too, so those
    names are inferred from the body and its callers, and are marked as OURS
    rather than passed off as upstream. The definition gets
    "LLM generated name (HIGH|GUESS), was <addr>: <reason>" and the short tag
    reads "[LLM:HIGH]" or "[LLM:GUESS]". A GUESS is tagged at every call site
    as well, not only the declaration, so a reader who meets the name anywhere
    knows it is inferred. "git grep '[LLM:'" lists every place the decomp
    leans on an inference.

The justification recorded for each individual name follows, one entry per
symbol, in the order the renames were made. Nothing is summarised away: these
are the per-commit reasons from the unsquashed history, with the boilerplate
that was identical in every one of them hoisted into the text above.

All 11 are defined in soh/src/overlays/actors/ovl_En_Ik/z_en_ik.c.

  * D_80A78470 -> sUnusedOffset
    deduced, confidence HIGH: the in-tree comment above it reads "//
    unused"; the Vec3f has no reference anywhere.

  * D_80A7847C -> sAxeQuadOffsets
    deduced, confidence HIGH: the four local-space corners of the axe's quad
    collider, transformed by the limb matrix each frame; the first two are
    also the tip and base of the blade trail.

  * D_80A784AC -> sShieldTri0Offsets
    deduced, confidence HIGH: the three local-space corners of the shield
    collider's first triangle.

  * D_80A784D0 -> sShieldTri1Offsets
    deduced, confidence HIGH: the three corners of the shield collider's
    second triangle, completing the quad.

  * func_80A761B0 -> EnIk_AllocColorDList
    deduced, confidence HIGH: allocates a small display list holding a prim
    and an env colour, bound to segments 8, 9 and 0x0A by the draw function.

  * func_80A76C14 -> EnIk_PlayIntroSfx
    deduced, confidence HIGH: fires the Iron Knuckle's wake-up, footstep,
    armour, finger and axe-wave sounds on their fixed frames of the
    introduction animation.

  * func_80A76DDC -> EnIk_PlayAxeSummonSfx
    deduced, confidence HIGH: plays the transform sfx that accompanies the
    axe appearing; EnIk_SpawnAxeSmoke calls it once the smoke has been
    thrown.

  * func_80A772A4 -> EnIk_PlayStaggerSfx
    deduced, confidence HIGH: plays the stagger sound for the Nabooru defeat
    cutscene; EnIk_SetupCsAction4 calls it.

  * func_80A772EC -> EnIk_PlayDeathSfx
    deduced, confidence HIGH: projects the actor's position through the view
    matrix and plays the death sound at that screen position;
    EnIk_SetupCsAction3 calls it.

  * func_80A774F8 -> EnIk_CsAction5
    deduced, confidence HIGH: the sixth entry of the cutscene action table:
    it kills the actor as soon as cue 4 goes away.

  * func_80A781CC -> EnIk_UpdateDefeated
    deduced, confidence HIGH: the update installed when the Iron Knuckle is
    beaten: once the player leaves cutscene mode it restores the normal
    update and draw, starts the Nabooru defeat cutscene, sets the
    battle-finished flag and enters cutscene action 3.

